{"url": "https://www.american.edu/provost/ogps/graduate-studies/Current-Graduate-Academic-Regulations.cfm", "final_url": "https://www.american.edu/provost/ogps/graduate-studies/current-graduate-academic-regulations.cfm", "status": 200, "method": "chromium", "fetched": "2026-11-13", "text": "Skip to main content\nShow\nMenu\nUNIVERSITY ANNOUNCEMENT\nNotice for AU employees: Learn More\nYou are here:\nAmerican University\nProvost\nOffice of Graduate & Professional Studies\nGraduate Studies\nGraduate Academic Regulations\nGraduate Studies Home\nProspective Students\nGraduate Application Deadlines\nStudent Services & Resources\nFunding & Aid\nNews\nCalendar of Events\nGraduate Academic Regulations\nAdvancement to Candidacy\nDissertations & Theses\nAbout\nThe Graduate Academic Regulations are maintained by the Office of Graduate & Professional Studies and approved by the American University Faculty Senate. They govern all American University students enrolled in graduate degree programs, in graduate certificate programs, or as graduate non-degree students.\nThe current Graduate Academic Regulations were ratified by Faculty Senate on May 7, 2025 and amended on February 19, 2026.\nView below (or download PDF)\nGraduate Academic Regulations\n1. Preamble\nThis document constitutes the American University\u2019s Academic Regulations of graduate degree and non-degree programs, as well as non-degree students, except those offered exclusively by the Washington College of Law. When enrolled in a joint program, students must satisfy the Academic Regulations that relate to both academic units in which their degree is housed. Section 11 delineates how the academic rules and regulations apply to graduate students enrolled in courses, certificates, or programs aligned with the four-term calendar. Students are expected to know and follow these regulations; ignorance of a regulation will not be accepted as an excuse for failure to act in accordance with it. Academic units and graduate programs may establish additional regulations for their students, but all students must follow these regulations. Based on a compelling rationale, an exception to a specific graduate academic regulation may be granted.\nSuggested changes to the Graduate Academic Regulations can come from any unit across campus including the Dean of Graduate and Professional Studies or designee within the Office of Graduate and Professional Studies (OGPS), or the faculty senate or one of its committees. Once a change is proposed, the Dean of Graduate and Professional Studies or the dean\u2019s designee initiates the change process whereby relevant units on campus including the academic units (via graduate associate deans, advisors, or other relevant parties) and the Office of the University Registrar evaluate the suggested changes. Once changes are properly vetted, the Dean of Graduate and Professional Studies or designee brings them to the appropriate faculty senate committee or the Faculty Senate Executive Committee, followed by the Faculty Senate for vote and approval. For the purposes of this document, the Office of Professional Studies (OPS) will be treated as an academic unit. The Dean of the Office of Professional Studies will be the Dean of Graduate and Professional Studies (DGPS). The Office of Professional Studies will designate a faculty or staff member with the responsibilities and authority of an Associate Dean. Similar to other academic units, the OPS may offer degree and certificate programs and may house degree-seeking and non-degree students.\n2. Admission Policies\n3. Evaluation of Academic Performance\n4. Degree Requirements\n5. Credit Requirements\n6. Requirements for a Graduate Certificate\n7. Requirements for a Master's Degree\n8. Requirements for a Doctoral Degree\n9. Requirements for Non-Credit Programs\n10. Registration Policies\n11. Four-Term Calendar\nAppendix: Glossary of Terms\nPetitions for Exception to Academic Regulations\nAdvisor Worksheets\nGraduate P